 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_b5Wd4YNZ9jMd7HH3",
   "name": "Arithmetic Sequences",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Arithmetic Sequences"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is an arithmetic sequence?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In an **arithmetic** **sequence,** the difference between consecutive terms in the sequence is constant\n\n  - This means a **common difference** is **added** to each term to get the **next term**\n- The **first term** of the sequence is denoted by $a$\n- The **common difference** is denoted by $d$\n\n  - For example, 1, 4, 7, 10, … is an arithmetic sequence with the rule *‘start at one and add three to each number’*\n  \n    - The **first term, **$a$***,***** **is 1\n    - The **common difference, **$d$**, **is 3\n- An arithmetic sequence can be\n\n  - **increasing **(positive common difference), or\n  - **decreasing **(negative common difference)\n- **Terms** in an arithmetic sequence can be referred to\n\n  - by the letter $u$ with\n  - a subscript corresponding to its place in the sequence\n  \n    - e.g.  $u_{1}=a$ is the first term, $u_{9}$ is the ninth term, $u_{n}$ is the $n$<sup>th</sup> term, etc."
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_85Xgrrh56QfsH6Qp",
   "name": "Arithmetic Series",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Arithmetic Series"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is an arithmetic series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When the terms of an **arithmetic sequence** are added together, that is known as an **arithmetic series**\n\n  - The **terms** (1st term, 2nd term, 3rd term, etc.) are **exactly the same** in the sequence and series\n  - But with series we're most interested in what happens when the terms are **added together**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find a term in an arithmetic series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The ***n***<sup>***th ***</sup>**term formula** for an arithmetic sequence is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$u_{n}=a+(n-1)d$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where $a$* *is the **first term**, and $d$ is the **common difference**\n- This is **not **given on the exam **formula sheet**, so make sure you know it\n- The formula allows you to find **any term** in the arithmetic series\n\n  - **Enter** the values of $a$, $r$ and $n$ and calculate the value of $u_{n}$\n- Sometimes you will be **given a term (**$u_{n}$**)** and asked to **find **$a$** or **$d$\n\n  - **Substitute** the information you have into the formula and solve the equation\n- Sometimes you will be **given two terms** and asked to **find both **$a$** and **$d$\n\n  - **Substitute** the information into the formula and set up a pair of **simultaneous equations**\n  \n    - Then solve the simultaneous equations"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the sum of an arithmetic series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- An **arithmetic series** is the sum of the terms in an **arithmetic sequence**\n\n  - For the arithmetic sequence 1, 4, 7, 10, … the arithmetic series is 1 + 4 + 7 + 10 + …\n- Use the following formula to find the sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic series:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`S subscript n equals n over 2 open square brackets 2 a plus space left parenthesis n minus 1 right parenthesis d close square brackets`*   *"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
